#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "U7")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U7.1 Fractions",[Prove $a/b=0$ in the fraction field of a domain exactly when $a=0$.],[Compare with the fraction $0/1$.],[Use the cross-multiplication definition.],[Equality with $0/1$ means $a dot 1=b dot 0$, hence $a=0$. Conversely a zero numerator gives that equality directly.],[Use the equivalence relation defining fractions.]),
 p(2,"E","U7.2 Minimal polynomials",[Find the minimal polynomial of $sqrt(7)$ over $QQ$ and a basis of its generated field.],[Use the polynomial $x^2-7$.],[It is irreducible by Eisenstein at seven.],[The minimal polynomial is $x^2-7$, since it is monic, vanishes at $sqrt(7)$, and is irreducible. Hence the extension has degree two and basis $1,sqrt(7)$.],[Verify irreducibility rather than merely exhibit a vanishing polynomial.]),
 p(3,"E","U7.1 Tower law",[If $[L:K]=3$ and $[M:L]=4$, find $[M:K]$. Can an intermediate field between $K$ and $M$ have degree five over $K$?],[Multiply the degrees.],[An intermediate degree must divide the total degree.],[The total degree is twelve. If $K subset.eq F subset.eq M$ had $[F:K]=5$, the tower law would give $12=[M:F]dot 5$, impossible.],[State the tower in each application.]),
 p(4,"E","U7.2 Power-basis arithmetic",[With $alpha^3=2$, simplify $alpha^5+alpha^4$ in the power basis.],[Reduce each exponent using $alpha^3=2$.],[The remaining powers should be below three.],[$alpha^5=2alpha^2$ and $alpha^4=2alpha$, so the result is $2alpha^2+2alpha$.],[Keep coefficients in the base field and powers below the minimal-polynomial degree.]),
 p(5,"C","U7.2 Inverses",[Find $(2+sqrt(3))^(-1)$ in $QQ(sqrt(3))$ and explain why every nonzero $a+b sqrt(3)$ has an inverse in this field.],[Multiply by the conjugate expression.],[The denominator is $a^2-3b^2$.],[The requested inverse is $2-sqrt(3)$ since the product is one. In general the inverse is $(a-b sqrt(3))/(a^2-3b^2)$. If the denominator were zero with a nonzero numerator, then $b!=0$ and $(a/b)^2=3$, contradicting irrationality of $sqrt(3)$. Thus the denominator is nonzero.],[Check the denominator cannot vanish for a nonzero element.]),
 p(6,"C","U7.2 Algebraicity",[Prove every element of a finite extension is algebraic.],[Use too many powers in a finite-dimensional vector space.],[If the degree is $d$, the $d+1$ elements $1,alpha,dots,alpha^d$ are dependent.],[Put $d=[L:K]$. For any $alpha in L$, the $d+1$ powers $1,alpha,dots,alpha^d$ are linearly dependent. This supplies coefficients $c_i in K$, not all zero, with $sum_(i=0)^d c_i alpha^i=0$. The corresponding nonzero polynomial annihilates $alpha$, so $alpha$ is algebraic.],[Explain why the polynomial is nonzero.]),
 p(7,"C","U7.2 Change of generator",[Find the minimal polynomial of $1+sqrt(2)$ over $QQ$.],[Isolate the square root and square.],[A quadratic with no rational root is irreducible.],[If $alpha=1+sqrt(2)$, then $(alpha-1)^2=2$, so $alpha^2-2alpha-1=0$. The polynomial $x^2-2x-1$ has roots $1±sqrt(2)$, neither rational, hence is irreducible and is the minimal polynomial.],[Show irreducibility and monicity.]),
 p(8,"C","U7.1 Fraction universal property",[Explain why the reduction map $ZZ -> FF_5$ cannot extend to a unital homomorphism $QQ -> FF_5$.],[Consider the inverse of five in $QQ$.],[A homomorphism would send $5 dot (1/5)=1$ to zero.],[Such an extension would send five to zero. Applying it to $5(1/5)=1$ yields $0 dot f(1/5)=1$, a contradiction in the nonzero field $FF_5$. This shows why the fraction-field extension theorem requires nonzero denominators to have nonzero images.],[Identify the exact relation whose image is impossible.]),
 p(9,"C","U7.2 Root adjunction",[Construct a field containing a root of $x^3+x+1$ over $FF_2$ and list a basis over $FF_2$.],[First check irreducibility using the two possible roots.],[Use the quotient by the polynomial.],[At zero and one the polynomial has value one, so as a cubic it is irreducible. Therefore $K=FF_2[x] slash (x^3+x+1)$ is a field. Writing $alpha=[x]$, the elements $1,alpha,alpha^2$ form a basis and $alpha^3=alpha+1$.],[Explain why the quotient is a field and why the powers are a basis.]),
 p(10,"A","U7.1 Compositum degree",[Prove $[QQ(sqrt(2),sqrt(5)):QQ]=4$ and find a basis.],[Show $sqrt(5)$ is not in $QQ(sqrt(2))$.],[Square a proposed expression $a+b sqrt(2)$.],[If $sqrt(5)=a+b sqrt(2)$ with rational $a,b$, squaring gives $5=a^2+2b^2+2a b sqrt(2)$, so $a b=0$. If $b=0$, then $a^2=5$; if $a=0$, then $b^2=5/2$. Neither is a rational square, as prime exponents in a rational square are even. Thus adjoining $sqrt(5)$ has degree two over $QQ(sqrt(2))$, and the tower law gives four. Products of the two quadratic bases yield $1,sqrt(2),sqrt(5),sqrt(10)$.],[Exclude both cases and invoke the basis form of the tower law.]),
 p(11,"A","U7.2 Transitivity of algebraicity",[Let $L/K$ be algebraic and let $alpha$ be algebraic over $L$. Prove $alpha$ is algebraic over $K$, even when $L/K$ is infinite.],[Only finitely many coefficients occur in an annihilating polynomial.],[Adjoin those coefficients to $K$ first.],[Let $c_0,dots,c_d in L$ be the coefficients of a nonzero polynomial annihilating $alpha$. The field $F=K(c_0,dots,c_d)$ is finite over $K$, since it is generated by finitely many algebraic elements. The element $alpha$ is algebraic over $F$, so $F(alpha)/F$ is finite. The tower law makes $F(alpha)/K$ finite. Every element of a finite extension is algebraic, so $alpha$ is algebraic over $K$.],[Do not apply a finite-degree tower law directly to the potentially infinite extension $L/K$.]),
 p(12,"A","U7.2 A primitive generator",[Show $QQ(sqrt(2)+sqrt(3))=QQ(sqrt(2),sqrt(3))$ and find the minimal polynomial of the sum.],[The reciprocal of the sum recovers the difference.],[Since $(sqrt(3)-sqrt(2))(sqrt(3)+sqrt(2))=1$, both roots can be recovered.],[Put $alpha=sqrt(2)+sqrt(3)$. Then $alpha^(-1)=sqrt(3)-sqrt(2)$, so $sqrt(3)=(alpha+alpha^(-1))/2$ and $sqrt(2)=(alpha-alpha^(-1))/2$. This proves equality of the fields. Squaring twice gives $(alpha^2-5)^2=24$, hence $alpha^4-10alpha^2+1=0$. The field has degree four by Lecture 13, so the minimal polynomial has degree four and must be this monic quartic.],[Use the degree to prove minimality; a vanishing quartic alone is insufficient.]),
)
